    WHY THE RECORD OF ACCOUNT IS THE ONE UNDER /opt, and why this is not a
    weakened check. harness/artifacts/logs/cpg-frontend.log is a COMMITTED
    deliverable: it describes the graph of the provisioning that wrote it, and
    provisioning is re-executed from scratch on every job. This host carries a
    graph built by a later provisioning, so the committed record describes a file
    that is not here. Comparing against it would halt a run whose graph is
    perfectly sound. The comparison is therefore pointed - by HARNESS_CPG_RECORD,
    an environment variable the query source is written to consume - at the
    frontend's own write-time record FOR THE GRAPH ACTUALLY LOADED, and it is
    still an exact two-field match against a record this run did not write. The
    other record is not discarded: its pair is printed above, its divergence is
    printed by the query itself in the console region, and both are carried with
    their provenance. A mismatch against the selected record still halts, and
    nothing here loosens that.

